# Protection flags for benchmark/corpus_allocsize/ -- the allocation-size
# integer-overflow family (CWE-190 -> CWE-122/787).
#
# HELD IDENTICAL ACROSS EVERY TARGET IN THIS FAMILY ON PURPOSE. The only thing
# that varies between targets is the ARITHMETIC THAT COMPUTES THE ALLOCATION
# SIZE inside new_vec(); everything else -- the menu, the record layout, the
# leak channel, the transfer clamp, the indirect call -- is byte-identical. A
# protection difference would confound that measurement.
#
# -fstack-protector-all  canary=ON. Nothing in this category touches a return
#                        address, so the canary must not be what stops the
#                        exploit -- and a family that left it off would not
#                        model the modern case.
# -pie -fPIE             PIE=ON. Makes a code-address leak MANDATORY: win()'s
#                        address is only known after show() has dumped a job
#                        record and disclosed its handler pointer.
# -Wl,-z,relro,-z,now    RELRO=FULL. The GOT is read-only, so "overflow the
#                        tiny chunk into free@got" is STRUCTURALLY unavailable.
#                        The writable code pointer this family aims at is the
#                        program's own: the `run` handler inside the adjacent
#                        job record on the heap.
# -O0                    the family's geometry IS the measurement. Two things
#                        must survive the compiler: the handler field must stay
#                        at a fixed offset inside the record and the call
#                        through it must stay an indirect call; and the
#                        allocation-size arithmetic must stay as written, in the
#                        width it was written in. At higher -O gcc is free to
#                        re-associate or widen `count * ELEM_SZ` and the target
#                        silently stops being in this category.
#
# NX is ON (default, no -z execstack).
#
# BUILT AND TESTED AGAINST
# ------------------------
#   glibc   2.35   (Ubuntu GLIBC 2.35-0ubuntu3.15)   <-- `ldd --version`
#   gcc     11.4.0 (Ubuntu 11.4.0-1ubuntu1~22.04.3)
#   kernel  6.8.0 x86-64, Ubuntu 22.04
#
# Two version-sensitive facts this family depends on, both glibc-specific:
#   * malloc(0) returns a VALID pointer to the minimum chunk (0x20 on x86-64),
#     which is what turns a wrapped-to-zero size into a tiny buffer rather than
#     into a NULL the program would reject;
#   * realloc(p, small) SPLITS IN PLACE and returns the same pointer when the
#     remainder is at least MINSIZE, which is what alloc_13 relies on.
# A later reader comparing results MUST check their own `ldd --version` first.
-fstack-protector-all
-pie
-fPIE
-Wl,-z,relro,-z,now
-O0
